<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Patch>

  <!-- ===========================================================================================
       Collapses Odyssey's category and vanilla's Ship category into one Space category, and adds the
       two orbital consoles vanilla leaves in Misc.

       <b>The whole thing is conditional on Odyssey being installed.</b> Without it there is no
       gravship content, so Ship on its own is a reasonable tab and merging two things into one would
       gain nothing while renaming a category players know. The condition is a test for Odyssey's own
       DesignationCategoryDef rather than for the DLC being active, because that def is exactly what
       this needs to exist before it can be emptied.

       <b>Both source categories are hidden, not removed.</b> They used to be removed, and the argument
       for it was that everything naming them had already been repointed by the operations above, so
       nothing was left to break. That holds for the defs in the game as shipped. It does not hold for a
       mod loaded after this one: a mod that adds a building to the Ship category names a category that
       is no longer there, which is an unresolved reference and a def error in someone else's mod caused
       by this one. Hiding keeps the reference resolvable.

       Nothing in the base game depends on their removal either way. DesignationCategoryDefOf names only
       Production, Floors and Zone, so no DefOf resolution breaks, and no vanilla code looks either
       category up by name.

       <b>What hiding costs.</b> A mod that adds buildings to Odyssey or Ship after this patch runs now
       puts them in a category the player cannot open, where removal would at least have produced a loud
       error. That is the trade: a silent gap in one mod's content against a def error in it. The gap is
       recoverable by the player disabling this mod; the error is not recoverable by anyone but its
       author.

       Order still matters within the sequence. Repoint first, hide second, so the repointing operations
       are matching on categories that are still exactly as their authors wrote them.

       ===========================================================================================
       <b>Every operation is guarded by exactly what it needs.</b> This is the same shape the storage tab
       patch was rewritten into, and for the same reason, so the reasoning is recorded once here as well
       rather than only in that file.

       An operation like Replace fails when its xpath matches nothing, and a failure anywhere in a
       sequence fails the sequence and prints a patch error naming this mod. Being inside a conditional
       that tested for the <i>category</i> does not protect an operation that needs a <i>member of</i>
       that category: the two are different questions, and another mod that repoints those members first
       makes the answers differ. RimWorld applies patches in load order, so which mod gets there first is
       not something this mod controls or can predict.

       The terrain operation below is the clearest case. It asks for TerrainDefs in the Odyssey category,
       and there is no rule saying that category has to contain terrain. If a future Odyssey update, or
       any mod, leaves it with none, an unguarded Replace there would fail on a completely healthy game.
       =========================================================================================== -->

  <Operation Class="PatchOperationSequence">
    <success>Normal</success>
    <operations>

      <li Class="PatchOperationConditional">
        <xpath>Defs[DesignationCategoryDef/defName="Odyssey"]</xpath>

        <match Class="PatchOperationSequence">
          <operations>

            <li Class="PatchOperationConditional">
              <xpath>Defs/ThingDef/designationCategory[text()="Odyssey"]</xpath>
              <match Class="PatchOperationReplace">
                <xpath>Defs/ThingDef/designationCategory[text()="Odyssey"]/text()</xpath>
                <value>Space</value>
              </match>
            </li>

            <!-- TerrainDef as well as ThingDef: gravship platforms and hull flooring are terrain, and
                 leaving them behind would keep the old category populated and therefore visible. -->
            <li Class="PatchOperationConditional">
              <xpath>Defs/TerrainDef[designationCategory[text()="Odyssey"]]</xpath>
              <match Class="PatchOperationReplace">
                <xpath>Defs/TerrainDef[designationCategory[text()="Odyssey"]]/designationCategory/text()</xpath>
                <value>Space</value>
              </match>
            </li>

            <li Class="PatchOperationConditional">
              <xpath>Defs/ThingDef/designationCategory[text()="Ship"]</xpath>
              <match Class="PatchOperationReplace">
                <xpath>Defs/ThingDef/designationCategory[text()="Ship"]/text()</xpath>
                <value>Space</value>
              </match>
            </li>

            <!-- These two replace the whole element rather than its text, because vanilla files them
                 under Misc and the element has to change value, not just content. Both belong here:
                 an orbital trade beacon and a comms console are how a colony talks to orbit.

                 Guarded on the element rather than on the def. A mod that removes a building's
                 designationCategory outright has taken it off the architect panel deliberately, and
                 putting it back under Space would be this patch overruling that decision. -->
            <li Class="PatchOperationConditional">
              <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="OrbitalTradeBeacon"]/designationCategory</xpath>
              <match Class="PatchOperationReplace">
                <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="OrbitalTradeBeacon"]/designationCategory</xpath>
                <value>
                  <designationCategory>Space</designationCategory>
                </value>
              </match>
            </li>

            <li Class="PatchOperationConditional">
              <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="CommsConsole"]/designationCategory</xpath>
              <match Class="PatchOperationReplace">
                <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="CommsConsole"]/designationCategory</xpath>
                <value>
                  <designationCategory>Space</designationCategory>
                </value>
              </match>
            </li>

            <!-- Replace or add, rather than always adding. A second hidden element on the same def is
                 not an error RimWorld reports; it just parses one of them and drops the other. -->
            <li Class="PatchOperationConditional">
              <xpath>Defs/DesignationCategoryDef[defName="Odyssey"]/hidden</xpath>
              <match Class="PatchOperationReplace">
                <xpath>Defs/DesignationCategoryDef[defName="Odyssey"]/hidden</xpath>
                <value>
                  <hidden>true</hidden>
                </value>
              </match>
              <nomatch Class="PatchOperationAdd">
                <xpath>Defs/DesignationCategoryDef[defName="Odyssey"]</xpath>
                <value>
                  <hidden>true</hidden>
                </value>
              </nomatch>
            </li>

            <!-- Ship gets its own existence test, unlike Odyssey, which the block above already
                 established. It is a Core category today and this patch has no say in whether it stays
                 one. -->
            <li Class="PatchOperationConditional">
              <xpath>Defs/DesignationCategoryDef[defName="Ship"]</xpath>

              <match Class="PatchOperationConditional">
                <xpath>Defs/DesignationCategoryDef[defName="Ship"]/hidden</xpath>
                <match Class="PatchOperationReplace">
                  <xpath>Defs/DesignationCategoryDef[defName="Ship"]/hidden</xpath>
                  <value>
                    <hidden>true</hidden>
                  </value>
                </match>
                <nomatch Class="PatchOperationAdd">
                  <xpath>Defs/DesignationCategoryDef[defName="Ship"]</xpath>
                  <value>
                    <hidden>true</hidden>
                  </value>
                </nomatch>
              </match>
            </li>

          </operations>
        </match>
      </li>

    </operations>
  </Operation>

</Patch>
